{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-35928",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+y=2\\,⟹\\,x=2-y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-y)^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+4+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{y}^{2}-4y+4 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4({y}^{2}-y+1) \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3 \\\\ 6z & =4{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+9 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the parabola $z=\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
